Linux Mounting
What is Mounting?

Mounting makes a filesystem available at a directory location within the Linux filesystem hierarchy.

Example:

/dev/sdb1
   ↓
mount
   ↓
/mnt/data

Once mounted, users and applications can access the filesystem through the mount point.

Useful commands
mount

Shows mounted filesystems.

findmnt

Shows mounted filesystems in a structured format.

lsblk

Displays block devices and their filesystem information.

Interview Answer

Mounting makes a filesystem accessible through a directory in the Linux filesystem hierarchy.

12. /etc/fstab

/etc/fstab contains filesystem mount configuration.

It can define filesystems that Linux should mount automatically.

Concept
/etc/fstab
↓
Filesystem mount configuration
↓
Automatic/persistent mounting
Interview Answer

/etc/fstab contains filesystem mount configuration and can define filesystems that should be mounted automatically.

Important

Incorrect changes to /etc/fstab can prevent systems from mounting filesystems correctly, so it should be edited carefully.

13. Linux sudoers

The sudoers configuration determines which users and groups are permitted to use sudo and under what conditions.

Main file:

/etc/sudoers

A safer editing method is:

sudo visudo
Why this matters

It provides controlled privilege escalation.

Example
sudo systemctl restart ssh
Interview Answer

The sudoers configuration defines which users or groups are authorized to use sudo and what commands they can execute with elevated privileges.

14. Linux Archives and Compression
tar

tar is commonly used to create archives containing files and directories.

Example:

tar -cvf backup.tar folder/

Extract:

tar -xvf backup.tar
gzip

gzip is used for compression.

Example:

tar -czvf backup.tar.gz folder/
Key Difference
tar
↓
Archive

gzip
↓
Compression
Interview Answer

Tar is primarily used to create and extract archives, while gzip is used to compress data.

15. Bash Conditions

Bash supports conditional statements that allow scripts to make decisions.

Example:

#!/bin/bash

if [ "$USER" = "root" ]; then
    echo "Root user"
else
    echo "Standard user"
fi

This demonstrates that Bash scripts can make decisions based on conditions.

Why useful?

Conditional logic allows scripts to:

Check whether files exist
Check user identity
Check command results
Respond differently to different conditions

For this roadmap, basic awareness is sufficient.
